memo:
**Halden rollout for the remaining order-service clusters**

**Decision:** Yes. Roll Halden out to the other six clusters this sprint, two a day as the rollout plan proposes. That takes about three working days.

**Why:** The case rests on our own trial, not on the vendor blog posts. Halden has run on eu-north and us-east for five weeks of normal traffic, including the month-end peak, using transaction pooling. Over that trial:

- connection set-up fell from 31 ms to 2 ms;
- connections per cluster fell from 2,900 to 180;
- p95 improved from 212 ms to 196 ms;
- the error rate held at 0.02%;
- Halden never restarted.

The other six clusters run the same service build and schema. A review of the order-service code found no feature that transaction pooling doesn't support. In particular, there is no LISTEN/NOTIFY, which is what forced one outside adopter to make changes.

**How sure we are:** Highly confident. The result was measured on two of our own eight clusters, which match the rest. Nothing in the evidence points the other way.

**Not verified:** The statement that the six remaining clusters match the trial ones comes with no evidence. Nobody has compared their traffic to the trial clusters. This doesn't change the decision. Rollback takes about five minutes per cluster: point the connection string back. Watch each pair of clusters as normal before moving on to the next.

**What we're not relying on:** The vendor and bookshop blog posts describe other companies' databases, clouds and languages. They agree with our results, but our own trial is what the decision rests on.
